50x50 torus, von Neumann, checkerboard Metropolis, 400 sweeps
T_c = 2 / log(1 + sqrt(2)) = 2.2692 

 temp magnetisation susceptibility
 1.00        0.9991          0.002
 1.50        0.9863          0.040
 2.00        0.9161          0.533
 2.27        0.7039         20.280
 2.60        0.1229         20.973
 3.50        0.0404          3.187

  Below T_c the mean spin settles on a non-zero magnetisation and
  above it the lattice is disordered. The susceptibility (the variance
  of the magnetisation, scaled by N) is two orders of magnitude larger
  at 2.27 and 2.6 than at 2.0 or 3.5: on a 50x50 lattice with a
  six-point sweep the finite-size peak sits just above T_c.
  Started ordered and equilibrated, which is how spontaneous
  magnetisation is measured. Quenching from a random start instead
  traps the lattice in striped metastable states at low T -- a
  finite-size artifact of the model, not of the grammar.
